// `io.inspect` IS THE THIRD CALLER OF `debugRendering`, AND THIS FIXTURE IS THE
// PAIR THAT PROVES IT RATHER THAN THE CLAIM THAT IT IS.
//
// `dbg_surfaces.nomi` established that a value has several renderings and that
// reaching for the nearest one passes every existing assertion. `io.inspect` was
// the one Debug SPELLING with no fixture, because it did not lower: `stdFreeCall`
// answered `io.print` directly and left `io.inspect` — its declared PAIR in
// std/io — to the stdlib index, which files every `<T>` declaration under
// `stdlib generic function` from the declaration's shape alone.
//
// THE TOUR IS WHAT FOUND THAT, and it is the reason this file exists. The corpus
// reaches this rendering through `Debug.inspect(x)` and `dbg`, both of which
// already lowered, so it never noticed. The tour reaches it through `io.inspect`
// in `modules-and-imports.md`'s FIRST runnable example and in
// `bindings-and-expressions.md`'s — the second chapter a reader reads.
//
// Every line below is a value on which the DEBUG rendering and the DISPLAY one
// disagree, printed through both spellings so the difference is a diff:
//
//	value                io.inspect says          io.print says
//	"hi"                 "hi"                     hi
//	Rev{zeta:1,alpha:2}  Rev{zeta: 1, alpha: 2}   Rev(...) — no Display impl
//	Custom{…}            CUSTOM-DEBUG             (no Display impl)
//	Meters(7)            <opaque Meters>          (no Display impl)
//
// The struct rows are FIELD ORDER (declaration versus sorted), which is why
// `Rev` declares `zeta` before `alpha`: with `Point {x, y}` the two orders are
// one string and this file would be vacuous.
// `TestIoInspect_DeclarationOrderIsNotSortedOrder` is the anti-vacuity guard, the
// same one dbg_surfaces.nomi carries for the same reason.
//
// THE ALIASED IMPORT IS NOT DECORATION. `stdFreeCall` keys on the CANONICAL
// module name, and the tour's own first example writes `import std/io as
// console` then `console.inspect(parsed)`. A version of this arm keyed on the
// local spelling passes every unaliased case and refuses that one — which is a
// bug std/io already had once, recorded in stdFreeCall's header for `o.print`.
import std/io as console

opaque type Meters Int

// zeta before alpha, deliberately. See above.
struct Rev {
    zeta: Int
    alpha: Int
}

struct Custom {
    zeta: Int
    alpha: Int
}

impl Debug for Custom {
    fn inspect(v: Custom): String {
        _ = v
        "CUSTOM-DEBUG"
    }
}

fn main() {
    // A String is the cheapest Debug/Display disagreement there is: Debug quotes
    // and Display does not, so these two lines differ by two characters and one
    // of them is the whole routing question.
    console.inspect("hi")
    console.print("hi")

    console.inspect(42)
    console.inspect(True)

    // DECLARATION order, not sorted order.
    console.inspect(Rev{zeta: 1, alpha: 2})
    // A hand-written impl Debug WINS. The assertion row's renderer ignores it.
    console.inspect(Custom{zeta: 1, alpha: 2})
    // An opaque type renders name-only. The row shows the payload.
    console.inspect(Meters(7))

    // Containers and prelude instances, composed from the same leaf renderings.
    console.inspect([Rev{zeta: 1, alpha: 2}])
    console.inspect((1, "two"))
    console.inspect(Some(42))
    none: Maybe<Int> = None
    console.inspect(none)
    console.inspect({a: 1, a0: 2})
    console.inspect(["a", "b"])
}
